---
/**
 * "How it was built" — la página de proceso del TFM, colgando de su ficha.
 *
 * Por qué existe separada de `work/tfm.md`: la ficha contesta *qué es* el
 * proyecto; ésta contesta *cómo se decidió*, que es lo que un PI no puede
 * leer en ningún otro portfolio. El material sale del decision log real del
 * proyecto, donde cada entrada guarda el razonamiento Y lo descartado.
 *
 * ⚠️ Sin cifras de resultado, y es deliberado: la tesis se defiende en enero
 * y los números son suyos hasta entonces. La página se sostiene sobre las
 * decisiones, que además es la parte que no caduca.
 *
 * Figuras: dibujadas desde los tokens (AX-8). La única estructura que
 * se muestra aquí es el flujo de la pipeline, y se dibuja por la misma razón
 * que las demás — se corrige en segundos cuando el diseño se mueve.
 */
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import PipelineFlow from "../../../components/PipelineFlow.astro";
import DecisionCard from "../../../components/DecisionCard.astro";
import { withBase } from "../../../utils/url";
---

<Base
  title="How the thesis was built"
  description="The design decisions behind an LLM-plus-deterministic-code pipeline for grapevine gene curation — each one with what it discarded and what it cost."
>
  <article class="proc">
    <div class="container proc__wrap">
      <a class="proc__back" href={withBase("/work/tfm")}>← Back to the thesis</a>

      <header class="proc__header">
        <span class="mono-label">Data-science thesis · how it was built</span>
        <h1>Decisions, and what they discarded</h1>
        <p class="proc__lede">
          The thesis page says what the pipeline does. This one says how it
          came to be that way — because the interesting part of building a
          system that mixes language models with code a scientist has to trust
          is not the architecture you end up with, it's the alternatives you
          turned down and the reasons that survived contact with the work.
        </p>
        <p class="proc__lede">
          Every decision below is in the project's log, numbered, with its
          reasoning and what it rejected. I'm not posting results before the
          defence, so there are no figures on this page. The decisions are the
          part that doesn't expire anyway.
        </p>
      </header>

      <PipelineFlow />

      <section class="proc__phase">
        <h2 class="proc__phase-title">Where the machine stops and the code starts</h2>

        <DecisionCard
          n="01"
          title="The model answers questions; it never assigns the level"
          discarded="Letting the model read the paper and return the evidence level directly — one call, far less plumbing."
          cost="Every question has to be written, calibrated and tested on its own, and a wrong boolean is now a bug with a location rather than a bad answer."
        >
          <p>
            The deliverable is an evidence level on a published six-tier scale,
            and a level is exactly the kind of judgement a reviewer will want to
            challenge. So the model never produces one. It answers a fixed set
            of structured yes/no questions about what the paper actually
            reports, and tested code turns those booleans into the level.
          </p>
          <p>
            The difference is not stylistic. A level that came out of a model is
            an opinion you can only re-obtain by asking again; a level derived
            by a function is a claim you can re-derive from stored booleans,
            audit line by line, and correct at the rule rather than at the
            output. It also means the mapping can be unit-tested, which an
            opinion cannot.
          </p>
        </DecisionCard>

        <DecisionCard
          n="02"
          title="Several signals, one deterministic rule — and it happened twice"
          discarded="A single call per gene, trusted as-is; and for the reference, the first identifier any one source returned."
          cost="More calls per gene and more code holding the votes, for an answer that is the same answer most of the time."
        >
          <p>
            The classifier runs the same keyed prompt several times and takes a
            majority per flag. Separately, the paper's identifier is resolved by
            agreement across independent sources rather than by trusting one.
            Those were designed months apart for unrelated problems, and they
            are the same pattern: <em>collect several signals, then let a
            deterministic rule decide</em> — never let the signal decide.
          </p>
          <p>
            The part I'd defend hardest is the tie-break. When the rule cannot
            decide, the system does not pick the more likely branch: it asks for
            the concrete fact. A pipeline that resolves its own ambiguity
            quietly is a pipeline whose error rate you cannot measure, because
            the cases it got wrong look exactly like the cases it got right.
          </p>
        </DecisionCard>

        <DecisionCard
          n="03"
          title="Keep the model's share small enough to name"
          discarded="An end-to-end model pass producing the whole row, which was the obvious first design and reads far better in a diagram."
          cost="The pipeline is a lot more code than a prompt, and every deterministic step is a step somebody has to maintain."
        >
          <p>
            Of the columns in an output row, only a small and enumerable set is
            model-generated: the booleans, and the few descriptive fields that
            genuinely need language understanding and cannot be derived by rule.
            Everything else — normalisation, resolution, metadata, the level
            itself — is code.
          </p>
          <p>
            That ratio is the honest answer to <em>how much of this is AI?</em>,
            and it is a design target rather than an accident. A model call
            cannot be replayed, so it cannot be tested; every one you remove is
            a part of the system that moves from unrepeatable to verifiable.
          </p>
        </DecisionCard>
      </section>

      <section class="proc__phase">
        <h2 class="proc__phase-title">Making it auditable by someone who wasn't there</h2>

        <DecisionCard
          n="04"
          title="The symbol is the identity; the identifier is the link"
          discarded="Renaming duplicated gene symbols to make them unique, which is what a database schema wants."
          cost="Every downstream step has to carry a compound key, and nothing can assume a symbol appears once."
        >
          <p>
            Gene families where several members share a symbol are not an edge
            case in this literature, they're routine. Renaming them would have
            made the tables tidy and the output wrong: the name in the paper is
            what a curator will search for, and a renamed one matches nothing.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the unit is the pair — symbol and identifier together — end to
            end, and family members stay separate rows under the name their
            paper gave them. Tidiness lost to fidelity, which is the right way
            round when a human has to recognise the output.
          </p>
        </DecisionCard>

        <DecisionCard
          n="05"
          title="Store the attempts, not just the answer"
          discarded="Storing the final row only — smaller, faster, and enough for the deliverable."
          cost="A log that grows much faster than the result it explains, and a schema that has to version what produced each attempt."
        >
          <p>
            Each attempt is recorded with the booleans it produced and the model
            and prompt that produced them, and a curator's corrections are
            recorded field by field rather than as a replacement row.
          </p>
          <p>
            The payoff arrives the first time a rule changes. Because the
            booleans are stored, a re-derivation is a function call over
            existing data — not a re-run against a model that may not answer the
            same way twice, and may not even be the same model by then. And
            because the human edits are separate from the machine's output, it
            is always possible to ask how often the two disagreed.
          </p>
        </DecisionCard>

        <DecisionCard
          n="06"
          title="One rule, one home — enforced by a test, not by discipline"
          discarded="Keeping a copy of the level-derivation logic next to each caller, which is faster to write and reads fine in review."
          cost="An import boundary to respect, and a test that fails for reasons that look pedantic until the day it doesn't."
        >
          <p>
            The function that turns booleans into a level had been copied, and
            the copies drifted. Nothing failed loudly: two paths through the
            system simply started producing different levels from the same
            evidence, which is the worst failure mode available — silent, and
            invisible in review.
          </p>
          <p>
            The fix was not a note in the contributing guide. The rule now has
            exactly one home and a test that pins it there, because a
            convention nobody can run is a convention that lasts until the next
            deadline.
          </p>
        </DecisionCard>
      </section>

      <section class="proc__phase">
        <h2 class="proc__phase-title">Where the human sits</h2>

        <DecisionCard
          n="07"
          title="Two gates, placed where being wrong is cheapest"
          discarded="A single review at the end, over the finished rows — the natural place to put a human, and the wrong one."
          cost="The expert's time is spent up front, on a screen full of identifiers, before there is anything satisfying to look at."
        >
          <p>
            The first gate sits immediately after detection: the author or a
            curator confirms the genes and their identifiers before anything is
            built on them. The second only fires when the earlier voting was
            unstable — a human is asked precisely when the machine has evidence
            it is unsure, and not otherwise.
          </p>
          <p>
            Everything downstream inherits the first gate's answer, so an error
            that survives it is an error propagated into every field that
            follows. Reviewing at the end would have caught the same mistakes
            after paying for all the work in between. Human review guards
            accuracy; the automation just does the lifting.
          </p>
        </DecisionCard>

        <DecisionCard
          n="08"
          title="A fixer nobody sees teaches nobody anything"
          discarded="Leaving silent normalisation in place — it worked, and it made the failure rate look better than it was."
          cost="A noisier gate. The expert now sees warnings about input that the system could have quietly repaired."
        >
          <p>
            The identifier is the primary deliverable and it arrives as
            unverified free text: a single paper can carry several incompatible
            spellings of the same reference. The normaliser repaired them
            silently, so the author never learned that their paper was
            ambiguous, and the system never learned how often it was guessing.
          </p>
          <p>
            The order of the fix is the point of it: <strong>flag first</strong>,
            then show confidence, then warn before bulk actions. Fixing first
            and reporting later would have preserved exactly the blind spot that
            made the problem invisible in the first place.
          </p>
        </DecisionCard>
      </section>

      <section class="proc__phase">
        <h2 class="proc__phase-title">Building it so another lab could run it</h2>

        <DecisionCard
          n="09"
          title="The pipeline produces files; it does not write to a database"
          discarded="Writing results straight into the catalogue from inside the request — one fewer moving part and an obviously nicer demo."
          cost="A handover step that has to be run deliberately, and a format that now has to be treated as an interface."
        >
          <p>
            The pipeline emits a table and a log, and stops. The component that
            loads them into the catalogue lives in the catalogue's own
            repository, not here — because a laboratory in another country
            running this tool does not want one lab's database schema inside it,
            and the moment that loader lives in the pipeline, every schema
            change in a repository we don't own becomes a change to the service.
          </p>
          <p>
            There was a second, harder reason, and it only appeared after
            reading the consumer's code: the database served to users is a copy,
            republished on a schedule. A row written directly into it is erased
            by the next sync, with no error anywhere. The boundary was already
            right on design grounds; that made it mechanical.
          </p>
        </DecisionCard>

        <DecisionCard
          n="10"
          title="If the format is the interface, version it so it can be refused"
          discarded="Freezing the format and documenting it — a promise, which is what most projects ship."
          cost="A version field to maintain and a reader that has to decide what it accepts."
        >
          <p>
            Once the loader lives elsewhere, the table-and-log format is the
            entire contract between two repositories. Freezing it is a promise;
            what makes it enforceable is a version carried in the log, so the
            reader can <strong>refuse</strong> a file it does not understand
            instead of half-loading it and leaving a partial state nobody
            notices.
          </p>
        </DecisionCard>

        <DecisionCard
          n="11"
          title="Scale by distributing the work, not by making this machine faster"
          discarded="Optimising throughput here — batching, a bigger card, more parallelism."
          cost="Portability becomes a requirement rather than a nice-to-have: providers behind one interface, models swappable, no assumption about the hardware."
        >
          <p>
            The step that would never have survived ten times the volume is the
            human gate, and no amount of GPU fixes that. So the answer to scale
            is architectural: each laboratory runs its own pass with its own
            compute and its own expert, and this installation's hardware stops
            being a ceiling and becomes a local condition.
          </p>
          <p>
            That reframing is what turned a hardware constraint from a blocker
            into a note. A better card improves <em>this</em> machine; someone
            else's machine was never this machine's problem.
          </p>
        </DecisionCard>
      </section>

      <section class="proc__phase">
        <h2 class="proc__phase-title">Two habits I'd keep on any project</h2>

        <DecisionCard
          n="12"
          title="A fallback with a date on it"
          discarded="'We'll rebuild it if it isn't working' — a fallback with no trigger, which is a hope."
          cost="Having to name a date before you know whether you'll need it, and then honour it."
        >
          <p>
            The browser gate had a written plan B and a deadline attached: if by
            a named month it was not working, it would be rebuilt on a simpler
            stack over the same API. The API contract was frozen early and kept
            in the record precisely so that fallback stayed cheap.
          </p>
          <p>
            A contingency without a trigger never fires — the date is what makes
            it a decision instead of a comfort.
          </p>
        </DecisionCard>

        <DecisionCard
          n="13"
          title="Open risks stay written down as open"
          discarded="Quietly carrying the two infrastructure risks until they were fixed, which is what the tidy version of a project looks like."
          cost="A state document that says, in writing, that something important is unmitigated."
        >
          <p>
            Two of this project's live risks are environmental rather than
            scientific — a network mount that can truncate files, and a virtual
            environment that turned out to share its interpreter with a shared
            machine's base install, so it isolates nothing. One has a written
            workaround. The other, honestly, has none yet, and the record says
            so in those words.
          </p>
          <p>
            I'd rather hand a supervisor a risk register with an unmitigated row
            in it than one that looks clean. A register with no bad rows is not
            a strong project; it's an unexamined one.
          </p>
        </DecisionCard>
      </section>

      <section class="proc__phase">
        <h2 class="proc__phase-title">The record this leaves behind</h2>
        <p class="proc__body">
          Every decision on this page exists as a numbered entry with its date,
          its reasoning, and — the field with no equivalent anywhere else — what
          it discarded. Version control tells me what changed and when. It does
          not tell me why, who originated the change, or what was considered and
          rejected, because a rejected approach never gets written down at all.
        </p>
        <p class="proc__body">
          Blocks of work in that record fold into each other when the shape of
          the problem changes, and the numbering keeps the gap rather than
          closing it, so a jump in the sequence is information instead of a
          mistake. It is the same instinct as everything above: preserve the
          evidence that a choice was made, because the choice is recoverable and
          the reasoning is not.
        </p>
        <a class="proc__link" href={withBase("/how")}>
          The method behind this, applied to everything else I build
          <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </section>

      <p class="proc__outro">
        None of this makes the model right. It makes the model's output
        something a scientist can check, correct, and reproduce — which is the
        only version of this work that belongs in a catalogue.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .proc {
    padding-block: var(--space-xl) var(--space-2xl);
  }
  .proc__wrap {
    max-width: 54rem;
  }
  .proc__back {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-lg);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .proc__header {
    padding-bottom: var(--space-lg);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xl);
    border-bottom: var(--border);
  }
  .proc__header h1 {
    margin-top: var(--space-sm);
    font-size: var(--text-2xl);
  }
  .proc__lede {
    margin-top: var(--space-md);
    font-size: var(--text-md);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  /* Cada fase agrupa decisiones que contestan a la misma pregunta de diseño:
     leerlas juntas es lo que enseña el criterio, no la decisión suelta. */
  .proc__phase {
    margin-top: var(--space-2xl);
  }
  .proc__phase-title {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-weight: var(--weight-medium);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-sm);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-lg);
    border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 30%, transparent);
  }
  .proc__body {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
    font-size: var(--text-md);
  }

  .proc__link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-xs);
    margin-top: var(--space-sm);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--accent-deep);
    transition: gap var(--dur) var(--ease), color var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }
  .proc__link:hover {
    color: var(--accent);
    gap: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .proc__outro {
    margin-top: var(--space-2xl);
    padding-top: var(--space-lg);
    border-top: var(--border);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-md);
    font-weight: var(--weight-medium);
  }
</style>
